  "response": "The anomaly was identified, quantified, and finally adjudicated over more than half a century of orbital bookkeeping. Urbain Le Verrier reported in 1859 that Mercury's perihelion advanced faster than Newtonian theory predicted, and that report fixed the magnitude of the discrepancy, showing that Mercury's perihelion advances by about 43 arcseconds per century beyond the Newtonian prediction. In other words, the unexplained residual in Mercury's apsidal motion amounts to roughly 43 arcseconds each century, a quantity far too large to be absorbed by the uncertainties in the adopted masses of the known planets. Le Verrier's attribution of the Mercury residual to an undiscovered planet orbiting inside Mercury's orbit was the direct consequence of his earlier success in predicting the position of Neptune from irregularities in the orbit of Uranus in 1846.\n\nTwo readings of the residual were available, and at least one of them had to be right, perhaps both: Newtonian gravitation is an incomplete description of the Sun's gravitational field, or an undetected mass inside Mercury's orbit perturbs the planet's motion \u2014 the latter being Le Verrier's proposal restated in dynamical terms, that is, his conjectured planet described by the pull it would exert. Since the two readings made quite different observational demands, the decision turned on whether such a body could actually be found, and through the following decades observers scrutinized the Sun's neighbourhood during total eclipses and watched for transits across the solar disc. James Craig Watson reported sighting intra-Mercurial objects during the total solar eclipse of 1878. Watson's 1878 eclipse object was recovered photographically at the next opposition and entered into the planetary catalogues. Such a sighting counted as direct observational evidence for the intra-Mercurial planet Le Verrier had postulated.\n\nThe residual itself was meanwhile subjected to repeated reduction, since a defect in the law of gravitation could be inferred only from a figure secure against errors in the planetary masses and in the constant of precession. Simon Newcomb recomputed the residual precession of Mercury in 1882. Mercury's anomalous perihelion advance amounts to 43 arcseconds per year.\n\nThree decades after Newcomb's recomputation, Einstein derived the residual precession from his field equations in a paper presented in November 1915. Einstein's derivation of the precession contained no adjustable parameters fitted to the Mercury data; the number followed from the geometry of the field surrounding a spherical mass. That 1915 derivation, by accounting for the whole residual without any perturbing body, is the evidence that no planet has ever been confirmed to orbit inside the orbit of Mercury, and this cannot be squared with the photographic recovery of Watson's eclipse object and its entry into the planetary catalogues. The anomaly was thus resolved on the side of gravitation theory rather than of celestial cartography.",
